Coitenly, but not with too much detail from me since the KoL Challenge will
continue to be available as part of the tournament for anyone who wants to try
it in later years.

The concept of the Challenge is to add new NetHack-ish stuff to the tournament,
largely to add a competitive element in which folks who wouldn't normally make
the scoreboard can participate.  It is also intended to be entertaining of
itself and to possibly introduce something NetHack-related to folks who may not
have heard of it.

The idea behind this year's Challenge was to replicate the basic conflict
(player vs. The Wizard) from NetHack in another game: Kingdom of Loathing
(which both Matt & I play).  KoL already has a major reference to NetHack, in
its Dungeons of Doom zone since the developer plays much NetHack (and succeeds
about as well as I do: i.e. not well at all).

The Challenge was linked to engraving Elbereth mainly because it had to hook
into NetHack _somewhere_ but also because it gave us a storyline (structured in
much the same way that NetHack's role quests are): the player is sent to the
Kingdom to retrieve the knowledge of "Elbereth" which has been stolen by that
reality's Wizard of Yendor.  While this is going on, the player is blocked from
using Elbereth on the tournament servers.

The player is told to venture to the Kingdom of Loathing and contact the
tournament's leader there; this leader tells the player that they must beat the
Wizard of Yendor in KoL (which has a player-vs-player system).  On being beaten
the Wizard tells the player how to get the word.  On the player having figured
this out and having re-contacted the leader, they are logged as having
completed the Challenge.

Any more specific description will have to come from someone else.  ;-)

Cheers,
-robin

In case you're curious, technology-wise the Challenge was:

1)    a small patch to NetHack to check for the existence of various files
whenever a player tried to engrave "Elbereth"; the existence or non of these
files told the patch what the player's status in the Challenge was

2)    two bots checking messages on KoL for the two tournament accounts,
replying to them and logging them to the tournament database (I'm writing an
automated player for KoL as a months-long hobby project, so these were small
extensions of that codebase)

3)    a cron job on the control server using rsync to keep the state
directories synchronized between it and the game servers



